---
// The measurement page: one workload measured three ways. What is compared is the shape each
// engine gives the same record, because that is what a buyer chooses between.
import Chart from '../components/Chart.astro';
import { engines, indexMemory, ceiling, source, type Row } from '../data/bench';

const label = (n: number) => (n >= 1_000_000 ? `${n / 1_000_000}M` : n >= 1000 ? `${n / 1000}k` : String(n));
const mib = (bytes: number) => bytes / (1024 * 1024);
const kb = (bytes: number) => bytes / 1024;

const seriesOf = (pick: (row: Row) => number) =>
  engines.map((engine) => ({
    name: engine.name,
    color: engine.color,
    points: engine.rows.map((row) => ({ label: label(row.records), value: pick(row) })),
  }));

const at = (id: string, records: number) =>
  engines.find((engine) => engine.id === id)!.rows.find((row) => row.records === records)!;

const ktema100k = at('ktema', 100_000);
const log100k = at('log', 100_000);
const sqlite100k = at('sqlite', 100_000);
const ktema1m = at('ktema', 1_000_000);
const log1m = at('log', 1_000_000);
const sqlite1m = at('sqlite', 1_000_000);
const ktemaRows = engines[0]!.rows;
const logMap1m = indexMemory.log[indexMemory.log.length - 1]!.bytes;
---

<p class="bench__lede">
  One record, three stores, on one machine. The record is three 60-byte addresses, an origin, a media type and a
  payload of about 55 bytes, which is what the engine's own bench writes. Each chart below measures the same content,
  so the differences are the formats.
</p>

<div class="bench__grid">
  <Chart
    title="Memory to answer by address"
    caption={`The index each engine has to hold. The bare log builds one map entry per record, and at five million records that map is ${mib(indexMemory.log_at_five_million).toFixed(0)} MB. The engine's line is its optional structural index, a fixed array of about ${kb(indexMemory.engine_bytes).toFixed(0)} kB that this benchmark does not build: the run opens without record maps, so it answers a box query by walking the log. A database keeps its index in a file, so the memory it holds is the page cache that file is read through, which follows the records until it reaches about ${mib(indexMemory.sqlite[indexMemory.sqlite.length - 1]!.bytes).toFixed(0)} MB, the size the cache was configured with.`}
    unit="MB"
    scale="log"
    decimals={2}
    series={[
      {
        name: 'Ktema, optional index',
        color: 'var(--accent)',
        points: indexMemory.log.map((point) => ({
          label: label(point.records),
          value: kb(indexMemory.engine_bytes) / 1024,
        })),
      },
      {
        name: 'Append log',
        color: 'var(--fg-2)',
        points: indexMemory.log.map((point) => ({ label: label(point.records), value: mib(point.bytes) })),
      },
      {
        name: 'SQLite',
        color: 'rgb(122, 121, 119)',
        points: indexMemory.sqlite.map((point) => ({ label: label(point.records), value: mib(point.bytes) })),
      },
    ]}
  />
  <Chart
    title="Volume size"
    caption="Linear for all three, as a log is. At a million records the engine's volume is 473 MB, the append log's 245 MB and the database's 279 MB."
    unit="MB"
    scale="log"
    decimals={1}
    series={seriesOf((row) => mib(row.volume_bytes))}
  />
  <Chart
    title="Write one record"
    caption="Appending one record once the volume is built."
    unit="µs"
    scale="log"
    decimals={2}
    series={seriesOf((row) => row.write_us)}
  />
  <Chart
    title="Open"
    caption="Making the volume answerable. The log rebuilds its address map here; the engine reads its segment trailers."
    unit="µs"
    scale="log"
    decimals={1}
    series={seriesOf((row) => row.open_us)}
  />
  <Chart
    title="One record by address"
    caption="One record fetched by its own address. The log holds the whole address map in memory, so its lookup is constant; the engine builds no map, because the path that runs without an operating system has nowhere to keep one, so it finds the record by reading the log. That read grows from 5.7 kB at a thousand records to 646 kB at a million, and the line follows the bytes. The flat line here and the rising line in the memory chart are one trade seen from two sides."
    unit="µs"
    scale="log"
    decimals={2}
    series={seriesOf((row) => row.point_read_us)}
  />
  <Chart
    title="A tenth of the records by address"
    caption="A contiguous tenth of the addresses, enumerated rather than counted: about 100 records at a thousand and about 100,000 at a million. The engine's path carries no index, so it walks the log."
    unit="µs"
    scale="log"
    decimals={0}
    series={seriesOf((row) => row.range_us)}
  />
  <Chart
    title="Bytes read from the medium to answer"
    wide={true}
    caption="The engine's channel counts the bytes it reads, which is what the times above follow: one point read costs 64 kB at a hundred thousand records and 646 kB at a million, and a tenth costs nine times the whole volume at a hundred thousand and eighty-six times at a million. The other two report no byte count, so this chart carries one engine."
    unit="MB"
    scale="log"
    decimals={2}
    series={[
      {
        name: 'Point read',
        color: 'var(--accent)',
        points: ktemaRows.map((row) => ({ label: label(row.records), value: mib(row.point_read_bytes ?? 0) })),
      },
      {
        name: 'A tenth, enumerated',
        color: 'var(--fg-2)',
        points: ktemaRows.map((row) => ({ label: label(row.records), value: mib(row.range_bytes ?? 0) })),
      },
    ]}
  />
</div>

<section class="bench__fit">
  <h2>What the three are</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th></th><th>Ktema</th><th>Append log</th><th>SQLite</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>What it is</td>
        <td>An addressable history, written where the event happened</td>
        <td>A record of what happened, and nothing else</td>
        <td>Rows a program queries and changes</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Host it needs</td>
        <td>None on the device tier; an OS on the host tier</td>
        <td>An OS and a filesystem</td>
        <td>An OS and a filesystem</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>How an address is found</td>
        <td>Computed from the record itself</td>
        <td>A map entry per record, rebuilt at every mount</td>
        <td>An index on disk, walked at each read</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Bytes per record, at 1M</td>
        <td>{ktema1m.bytes_per_record}</td>
        <td>{log1m.bytes_per_record}</td>
        <td>{sqlite1m.bytes_per_record}</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Index the volume holds, at 1M</td>
        <td>{kb(indexMemory.engine_bytes).toFixed(0)} kB, optional; not built in this run</td>
        <td>{mib(logMap1m).toFixed(0)} MB, one entry per record</td>
        <td>{mib(indexMemory.sqlite[indexMemory.sqlite.length - 1]!.bytes).toFixed(1)} MB, page cache</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Append one record, at 100k</td>
        <td>{ktema100k.write_us.toFixed(1)} µs</td>
        <td>{log100k.write_us.toFixed(1)} µs</td>
        <td>{sqlite100k.write_us.toFixed(1)} µs</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Open, at 100k</td>
        <td>{(ktema100k.open_us / 1000).toFixed(2)} ms</td>
        <td>{(log100k.open_us / 1000).toFixed(2)} ms</td>
        <td>{(sqlite100k.open_us / 1000).toFixed(2)} ms</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>One record by address, at 100k</td>
        <td>
          {ktema100k.point_read_us.toFixed(2)} µs, reading {(kb(ktema100k.point_read_bytes ?? 0)).toFixed(0)} kB
        </td>
        <td>{log100k.point_read_us.toFixed(2)} µs</td>
        <td>{sqlite100k.point_read_us.toFixed(2)} µs</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>A tenth, by address, at 100k</td>
        <td>
          {(ktema100k.range_us / 1000).toFixed(0)} ms, reading {((ktema100k.range_bytes ?? 0) / ktema100k.volume_bytes).toFixed(1)} times the volume
        </td>
        <td>{(log100k.range_us / 1000).toFixed(2)} ms</td>
        <td>{(sqlite100k.range_us / 1000).toFixed(2)} ms</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Where it stops</td>
        <td>A volume of 4 GiB, about {ceiling.records.toLocaleString('en-US')} records</td>
        <td>When the address map no longer fits the memory it has</td>
        <td>Where its filesystem does not exist</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Right tool when</td>
        <td>The host has no operating system and the history is the product</td>
        <td>The only question is what happened, on a host of a few kilobytes</td>
        <td>The data changes and the questions are not known in advance</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</section>

<section class="bench__fit">
  <h2>Reading the times</h2>
  <p>
    The host rows are the engine's reference path, opened without record maps. That is the device's path: it reads
    the medium in bytes, keeps no block cache, and builds no index, so a box query walks the log. A tenth of the
    addresses at a hundred thousand records reads 9.5 times the volume, which is where its time comes from. The
    section below prices the other path.
  </p>
  <p>
    The append log and the database are at their best on a host, with an operating system, a filesystem and a warm
    page cache. Neither of them runs on a part with no operating system, and the log's address map stops fitting long
    before the volumes it is meant to serve. The engine's case is that tier.
  </p>
  <p>
    The append figure has the widest spread of the four. Repeated runs at a hundred thousand records put it between
    12 and 18 µs for both the engine and the database, while the other three stayed within a tenth of their value.
    Each row is one median of one run.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="bench__fit">
  <h2>The path this page does not measure</h2>
  <p>
    A volume opens two ways. The reference path builds nothing and answers by reading the log, which is what every
    number above measures. The cached path builds the record maps, at 394 to 406 bytes a record on a 32-bit target,
    and carries the structural index at about 354 kB, so a read is served from memory rather than from the medium. A
    host, or a part with memory to spare, can take that path. A part with 64 kB cannot, and that is the reason this
    page measures the reference path: it is the one that runs in both places.
  </p>
  <p>
    Both paths hold the same record in the same format, so the choice does not change what the volume is. It changes
    where the cost lands, on the medium or in the memory. What stays the same either way is the bytes each record
    takes, the 4 GiB a volume can address, and the record being written where the event happened.
  </p>
</section>

<p class="bench__source">
  {source.engine} and {source.peers} · {source.host} · {source.recorded} · median of {source.samples} samples ·
  SQLite {source.sqlite}. The append log here is a fixed-width frame per record plus an address map, which is what an
  integrator writes without a database. A single volume stops at 4 GiB because the format's offsets are 32 bits.
</p>
